A||N||5||cscougar||Welding Manifolds||||1||
Z||000000||cscougar||11-21-2001||11:18 AM||||My daughter has a 86 V-6 that has a cracked manifold on the trunk side.  It is a small crack and I was wondering if its possible to weld the crack with the manifold on the car instead of having to remove it..  any ideas?<P>Thanks<P>Ted||||reg||1
Z||000001||watts||11-21-2001||12:29 PM||||Partially depends on exatly where the crack is (I know my MIG head isn't that flexible!).  Having it still bolted to the head DOES help to prevent warpage of the flange mind you.<P>Access is going to be the main concern though which will depend on thing like if your welder person has a hoist, etc.<BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||wkayl||11-21-2001||02:05 PM||||This was welded on the car.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/welded.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||West Coast Fiero||11-21-2001||02:50 PM||||welding is of course acceptable as a last resort, however the strength of the weld is negligable due to the severe environment that the stainless manifolds have been in since the day off the assyembly line ( CO, rust, etc. ) <P> to increase the longitivity of the weld, take off the manifold and prep it by either beveling the area to be welded, or have them sand blasted or wire brushed<P><P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000004||fastback87||11-21-2001||03:28 PM||||I took both of mine off and had them welded.  They work great now.  You need to make sure that wherever you take them that they use stainless wire and if possible grind the gasket surface back flat to help prevent future stress fractures.  To remove the rear (trunk side) it will only take like 30-45min and you can do it from the top.  To remove the rear, you just have to jack up the car high enough to get under it and then get a couple of long extensions with a u-joint socket and you will have them off within about 2 hours.  I also figured out a way to drill out all the exhaust manifold bolts without removing the heads.  Thank god for right angle drills....I hope this helps a little.||||reg||10
Z||000005||Mickey_Moose||11-21-2001||07:37 PM||||once welded you should also have them ceramic coated. The coating retains the heat for a longer time and does not allow the manifolds to cool too rapidly...the rapid heating and cooling of the mainfolds are part of a reason why they crack (especially with stainless steel)<P>my 2 cents...<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||1
